{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 1.4 --platform codex && sed -n '1,240p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/prd.md && sed -n '1,240p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/design.md && sed -n '1,200p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/implement.md && python3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py start .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup && python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 2.1 --platform codex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 1.4 --platform codex && sed -n '1,240p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/prd.md && sed -n '1,240p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/design.md && sed -n '1,200p' .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup/implement.md && python3 ./.trellis/scripts/task.py start .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup && python3 ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 2.1 --platform codex\"","aggregated_output":"# Reliable teardown cleanup\n\n## Goal\n\nEnsure Flask teardown callbacks and signals all run before aggregated teardown errors are raised.\n\n## Confirmed Facts\n\n- `Flask.do_teardown_request` and `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext` currently stop at the first callback or signal receiver error (`src/flask/app.py`).\n- `AppContext.pop` invokes request teardown before app-context teardown and resets the application context in a `finally` block (`src/flask/ctx.py`).\n- Flask supports Python 3.10 and newer; exception groups are available beginning with Python 3.11 (`pyproject.toml`).\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Attempt every request teardown callback, including blueprint and app callbacks in their existing order, even if prior callbacks fail.\n- Attempt every app-context teardown callback even if prior callbacks fail.\n- Attempt every receiver of the request and app-context teardown signals even if callbacks or earlier receivers fail.\n- Raise collected teardown errors only after the relevant cleanup has completed. On Python 3.11+, preserve multiple errors in an exception group; on Python 3.10, raising the first collected error is acceptable.\n- Restore Flask context-local state before a teardown error leaves `AppContext.pop`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A request-context pop runs all failing and non-failing request teardown callbacks and teardown signal receivers, then reports the teardown failures.\n- [ ] An app-context pop runs all failing and non-failing app teardown callbacks and teardown signal receivers, then reports the teardown failures.\n- [ ] A request-context pop continues into application-context teardown when request teardown fails, preserving all teardown failures where exception groups are available.\n- [ ] `flask.current_app` is unbound after a failed context pop.\n- [ ] Existing public app-context tests pass.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Changing the order of teardown callbacks or signal receivers.\n- Changing non-teardown signal behavior or unrelated request cleanup semantics.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Keep `prd.md` focused on requirements, constraints, and acceptance criteria.\n- Lightweight tasks can remain PRD-only.\n- For complex tasks, add `design.md` for technical design and `implement.md` for execution planning before `task.py start`.\n# Design: Reliable teardown cleanup\n\n## Boundary\n\nThe behavior lives in `Flask`'s two teardown dispatch methods and in `AppContext.pop`, which owns the final context reset. Tests belong with the existing app- and request-context teardown tests.\n\n## Design\n\n`Flask` will use a small shared private dispatcher that executes a supplied sequence of teardown invocations while appending `BaseException` instances to one caller-owned list. The request and app-context teardown methods will add both registered callbacks and eligible signal receivers to that dispatcher, retaining their current callback order and passing the same `exc` value.\n\nEach public teardown method uses a local collector when called directly and raises it only after its work finishes. `AppContext.pop` supplies one collector to both methods, so request and application teardown failures are raised together only after the context variable is reset. A private raiser emits a `BaseExceptionGroup` when available and more than one failure was captured; Python 3.10 falls back to the first error.\n\n## Compatibility and Risk\n\nThe signal receiver loop mirrors Blinker `send`'s sender filtering through `receivers_for` and preserves Flask's async adaptation through `ensure_sync`. The helper is deliberately local to `Flask`, because both affected paths share application-owned synchronization and error policy.\n# Implementation Plan\n\n1. Add a private teardown dispatcher and error raiser in `src/flask/app.py`.\n2. Route request and app-context callbacks plus teardown signal receivers through the dispatcher.\n3. Share an error collector through `AppContext.pop` and raise after context-local restoration.\n4. Add regression tests for callbacks, signal receivers, aggregation, and context reset.\n5. Run the provisioned app-context test command and focused request-context tests.\n\n## Rollback Point\n\nThe change is isolated to teardown dispatch and its tests; reverting those source and test edits restores current short-circuit behavior.\n\u001b[0;31mError: implement.jsonl and check.jsonl have no curated entries\u001b[0m\nSub-agents (implement/check) would run with zero spec context.\n  Curate:  python3 .trellis/scripts/task.py add-context .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup implement <path> \"<why>\"\n  Verify:  python3 .trellis/scripts/task.py validate .trellis/tasks/09-20-reliable-teardown-cleanup\n  Intentionally empty? Re-run start with --allow-empty-context\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
